1
00:00:00,260 --> 00:00:09,380
Well, imagine a government, you know, loudly claiming they have this state-of-the-art digital school system, but then you actually walk into a primary school classroom, and the teacher is still, uh,

2
00:00:09,540 --> 00:00:11,840
tallying attendance with a pencil on a scrap of paper.

3
00:00:12,460 --> 00:00:16,260
Yeah, it is a very comforting picture they paint in those press releases.

4
00:00:16,820 --> 00:00:19,740
I mean, we all want to believe the official PR reflects reality,

5
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:22,900
but you really can't just read the reports.

6
00:00:23,140 --> 00:00:29,800
Right. You have to ask the operators in writing and actually demand the documents behind every single answer.

7
00:00:30,240 --> 00:00:32,680
Which brings us to our mission for today's deep dive.

8
00:00:33,220 --> 00:00:39,760
We are looking at a specific toolkit, so this framework of five questionnaires used to assess a nation's true digital footprint.

9
00:00:40,100 --> 00:00:46,740
Exactly. And we will unpack how this framework cuts through all that noise by demanding undeniable evidence.

10
00:00:47,300 --> 00:00:51,420
And we'll look at a practical example of how it maps a country's actual capabilities.

11
00:00:51,980 --> 00:00:59,020
But, um, I have to ask, how do you even begin to map an entire country's digital footprint without just getting completely overwhelmed by the scale of it?

12
00:00:59,560 --> 00:01:02,540
Well, it helps that the framework is built on the reference architecture.

13
00:01:02,980 --> 00:01:06,620
So it basically breaks the landscape down into five specific domains.

14
00:01:07,040 --> 00:01:10,700
Which are? You've got governance and policy, which they call QGOV;

15
00:01:11,640 --> 00:01:13,720
access, or QACC;

16
00:01:14,560 --> 00:01:16,740
digital data, so QDAT;

17
00:01:17,440 --> 00:01:19,460
interoperability, QINT;

18
00:01:20,200 --> 00:01:23,080
and finally, digital identity or QIDN.

19
00:01:23,740 --> 00:01:25,820
Oh, okay. So they have codes for each of them.

20
00:01:26,240 --> 00:01:31,100
Yeah. And together, these contain ninety-three questions that are grouped by twenty-six subcomponents.

21
00:01:31,340 --> 00:01:33,160
Wow, that is incredibly specific.

22
00:01:33,440 --> 00:01:41,500
It is. And this structure actually mirrors a desk review of the public record, so the on-the-ground reality can be set right beside the official claims.

23
00:01:42,520 --> 00:01:51,220
Asking ninety-three questions sounds great, honestly, but how do you stop government units from just, you know, giving overly optimistic answers and claiming all their systems are perfectly

24
00:01:51,260 --> 00:01:51,780
integrated?

25
00:01:52,120 --> 00:01:53,480
By demanding the receipts.

26
00:01:53,740 --> 00:01:55,300
Ha. Right, like a tax audit.

27
00:01:55,760 --> 00:01:56,840
Exactly like that.

28
00:01:57,520 --> 00:02:00,140
The questionnaires don't ask respondents to just score themselves.

29
00:02:00,340 --> 00:02:05,700
Instead, every question asks for three things: describe, indicate, and provide.

30
00:02:06,100 --> 00:02:09,979
Describe, indicate, provide. Okay. Break that down for me.

31
00:02:10,500 --> 00:02:12,260
First, they describe the current situation.

32
00:02:12,820 --> 00:02:13,000
Then?

33
00:02:13,720 --> 00:02:19,320
They indicate the format, like is it on paper, is it a spreadsheet or an actual database system?

34
00:02:19,660 --> 00:02:19,940
Got it.

35
00:02:20,640 --> 00:02:21,220
And provide?

36
00:02:21,780 --> 00:02:25,260
They have to provide a blank copy of the form or the screen they actually use.

37
00:02:25,820 --> 00:02:28,100
Oh, wow. So they can't just say, uh, "We have a database."

38
00:02:28,420 --> 00:02:37,400
Right. An answer with documentary evidence counts for way more, and crucially, just admitting, "We do not have this," is considered a highly useful answer.

39
00:02:38,020 --> 00:02:40,800
I bet. Uh, how long does that whole process usually take?

40
00:02:41,440 --> 00:02:46,100
The process is supported by two guides, one for the respondent and one for the facilitator,

41
00:02:46,740 --> 00:02:48,960
and it typically takes about thirteen working days.

42
00:02:49,480 --> 00:02:53,760
Okay. So what does it look like when a country actually answers one of these data questions?

43
00:02:54,560 --> 00:02:59,080
Well, the framework uses this fictional country of Progressa as an example.

44
00:02:59,320 --> 00:03:07,560
Okay, Progressa. Yeah. So when they were asked if there is one authoritative record for each student, which is question D2.2, by the way,

45
00:03:08,540 --> 00:03:10,480
the reality was highly fragmented.

46
00:03:10,660 --> 00:03:11,740
Wait, really? What did they find?

47
00:03:12,420 --> 00:03:15,160
The learner registry only served the exam authority.

48
00:03:15,780 --> 00:03:18,600
Primary schools just kept class registers on paper.

49
00:03:18,960 --> 00:03:23,220
Just paper. Yeah, and they only sent totals by grade and sex once a year.

50
00:03:23,820 --> 00:03:25,740
Oh my gosh, that is a huge disconnect.

51
00:03:26,060 --> 00:03:33,100
It gets worse. Progressa's main school information system, PMIS, actually held no individual learner records at all.

52
00:03:33,560 --> 00:03:36,160
Wow. How did they definitively prove that though?

53
00:03:36,700 --> 00:03:43,540
The evidence they provided to prove this was their blank annual school return form and the PMIS data dictionary.

54
00:03:44,040 --> 00:03:47,220
Ah, because the blank form wouldn't have a spot for individual names.

55
00:03:47,280 --> 00:03:47,980
The proof is right there.

56
00:03:48,380 --> 00:03:48,820
Exactly.

57
00:03:49,440 --> 00:03:51,980
True digital assessment means ignoring the PR,

58
00:03:52,700 --> 00:03:55,220
asking the people who actually run the systems,

59
00:03:55,840 --> 00:03:59,200
and requiring strict documentary proof for every single answer.

60
00:03:59,580 --> 00:04:06,620
That is fascinating. If you applied this rigorous describe, indicate, provide framework to the workflows in your own organization today,

61
00:04:07,100 --> 00:04:11,860
you have to wonder how many of your daily processes would actually have the documentation to back them up.
